            <ArticleTitle>Pilot study: does lower extremity night splinting assist in the management of painful peripheral neuropathy in the HIV/AIDS population?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>368-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1545109710373828</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The pain associated with peripheral neuropathy (PN) observed in patients living with HIV/AIDS represents a difficult complication to manage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 22 participants with a diagnosis of PN were recruited to assess the effects of using night splints on pain and sleep quality.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">For 3 weeks, the participants were instructed to use bilateral night splints and given an exercise regimen for 3 additional weeks. Scores from pain and sleep questionnaires were analyzed using repeated measures analysis of variance (ANOVA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The change in pain scores (F(1.16) = 13.41,η(2) =.456, P = .002) and sleep index scores (z = -2.69, P = .004, 1-tailed) was found to be significant following the use of night splints. No difference was found with the use of the exercises.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of night splints in HIV-infected patients with PN represents an additional tool for managing pain and improving sleep.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
